course: dictionary
lesson: 12379
title: Crystallisation: when things grow into shapes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A crystal is a solid with a neat, regular shape, like a tiny sugar cube or a sharp salt grain. Crystallisation is the process that makes crystals form. «After a week on the windowsill, the salt water had gone, and crystallisation had left tiny white crystals in the dish.» The water disappeared, and the salt was left behind in crystal shapes.
explain: Look for the clues around the word. The sentence mentions water disappearing and white crystals left in a dish. So crystallisation must be the way the crystals appeared. Try your meaning in the sentence: «the way crystals form» fits, so you are on the right track.
explain: Words can also have a second meaning. «After many talks, the idea slowly went through crystallisation until everyone could see the plan.» There are no crystals here. The idea became clear and definite, like a plan that finally takes solid shape. Check the words around the word to see which meaning the writer chose.
fact: Crystallisation means the process of crystals forming.
fact: It often happens when a salty or sugary solution cools, or when the water evaporates.
fact: Snowflakes and frost also form by crystallisation.
fact: In science, crystallisation is a way to get a solid back from a solution.
fact: «Crystallisation» can also mean an idea or plan becoming clear and definite.
exercise: «After the water evaporated, crystallisation left tiny white crystals in the dish.» What is crystallisation here? | The name of the dish | A kind of cold weather that freezes rivers | *The process of crystals forming as the water goes | A way of breaking crystals into dust
exercise: Sam says snowflakes are made by crystallisation. Is he right? | No, snowflakes are just melted ice | No, the wind makes all snowflakes | *Yes, frost and snowflakes can form this way | No, only sugar can crystallise
exercise: «The idea slowly went through crystallisation until everyone could see the plan.» What does crystallisation mean here? | Shouting the idea loudly | Forgetting the plan | Losing the idea completely | *The way an idea becomes clear and definite
test: Which home experiment can show crystallisation? | Stir sugar into cold milk and drink it at once | Freeze sugar in a bag and shake it | Put sugar in the oven until it burns | *Dissolve sugar in warm water, pour it in a dish and leave it for a few days
test: Mia says crystallisation is when crystals melt away. Is she right? | *No, crystallisation is when crystals form | No, crystallisation means breaking things into powder | Yes, but only when it is very hot | Yes, crystals always melt in crystallisation
exam: What must happen for crystals to form from salt water? | The crystals are heated until they burst | The water freezes into salt | *The water cools or evaporates, and the salt is left as crystals | The salt melts and turns into water
